db/ref 17592186446085 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446087 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446086 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446087 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446087 1077 "selection" 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446087 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446084 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446087 1079 #uuid "f3a6a40a-0b17-512b-bf03-9bbe6e406509" 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446087 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446088 1069 "q-72188" 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446088 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region that lies outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and inside the square defined by the lines $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=-1$ and $y=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446088 1072 #uuid "fd6674b6-1720-503b-8769-c73b368922ec" 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446088 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/e7/e762e510b690d65dfc8365d041c734d7b16611411004723a2abdbb74bfb41cc3.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/b7/b72bb25ce1dcce3dc7792bbbcb8a878dde14df3a90c9243a3c75ac26a19aaa47.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/eb/eb34633d98011ed7050694c722bc7c736a95939783efec3321d95517695642e2.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446089 1058 #edb/ref 17592186446092 13194139534969 17592186045417 true] [17592186446089 1062 #uuid "24b1c7cb-ecec-5845-8416-fdfafa79a79d"